# Default (2026-10-17, audit CR-2): text files are LF in the index AND the working tree, whatever a machine's
# core.autocrlf says. Without it, line endings depended on each checkout (the laptop's system gitconfig says
# input, its .git/config says false), and twelve working-tree files sat CRLF-dirty for weeks unseen.
# Every -text rule below still wins: those files are kept byte for byte.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Carved OpenRocket sources must remain byte-identical to upstream:
# no EOL conversion, ever (the carve script sha256-compares against upstream).
# (See the README's Building section for the carve workflow.)
engine-java/src/carved/** -text
engine-java/patches/** -text

# A hand-written RockSim engine file with CRLF line ends ON PURPOSE: the XML
# parity tests (packages/app/src/services/xmlParse.golden.test.ts) read it to
# prove both parsers normalise CR/CRLF the same way. No EOL conversion.
packages/app/src/services/__fixtures__/synthetic-ex-labs.rse -text

# Simulator data files exactly as thrustcurve.org serves them (motor-digest.test.mjs
# pins desktop OpenRocket's digests of them). Kept byte for byte; no EOL conversion.
packages/app/scripts/fixtures/motor-digest/** -text

# A RockSim design with CRLF line ends as committed: xml-parity/chrome-golden.json was recorded from these
# bytes, so they stay as they are (2026-10-17, audit CR-2). No EOL conversion.
packages/app/src/services/__fixtures__/ambiguous-motor-mounts.rkt -text
